    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait sorry you cut off at the end there. what do you mean by \"think of it like a goblin's voice\"? i just wanted to know about the vibration part for my exam.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, no worries at all! The goblin analogy was just a bit too enthusiastic for a serious exam question, but don't worry, the core mechanics are still there.\n\nTo answer your exam question directly: The difference between /s/ and /z/ lies entirely in the **vocal folds** (your vocal cords).\n\nWhen you produce **/s/** (unvoiced), your vocal folds are pulled apart and held in a relaxed state. They are like two goblins who have just finished a long, intense hug and are now standing far apart in a field, not touching at all. Because they aren't touching, no vibration occurs. The air simply rushes through the narrow gap between your tongue and your alveolar ridge, creating that sharp, hissing noise.\n\nWhen you produce **/z/** (voiced), your vocal folds come together and vibrate rapidly as air passes through them. Think of it like a pair of goblins who are constantly bumping into each other in a crowded room, creating a buzzing vibration. This vibration adds a \"hum\" to the sound, which is why /z/ sounds deeper and richer than /s/.\n\nSo, for your exam:\n*   **/",
      "source": "target",
